    <div class="service-content">
      <p>When rates rise and buyers get cautious, pricing becomes the most important decision a seller makes. A home priced even a few percent off the market can sit, collect price drops, and end up selling for less than if it had been priced correctly from day one.</p>
      <p>I'm Dan Byrne, Senior Partner &amp; Chief Appraiser at Las Vegas Appraisal Co. I've seen this play out many times, whether the seller is working with a listing agent or going FSBO.</p>

      <h2>Start With Your Motivation</h2>
      <p>Your reason for selling shapes your pricing strategy. Some sellers can wait for the right buyer. Others can't:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Divorce:</strong> a settlement may require a sale on a set timeline. <a href="/blog/divorce-appraisal-nevada">Learn how appraisals work in Nevada divorces.</a></li>
        <li><strong>Death of an owner:</strong> an estate often needs to sell promptly and fairly for all heirs. <a href="/blog/date-of-death-appraisal-nevada">See how date of death appraisals work.</a></li>
        <li><strong>Bankruptcy or financial strain:</strong> time is a cost.</li>
        <li><strong>Job relocation:</strong> a start date or closing deadline limits your window.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If you're a motivated seller, an unrealistic asking price works against you. If you have flexibility, you still need to know where the market actually is.</p>

      <h2>The Risk of "Testing the Market"</h2>
      <p>A common approach is to list high and see what happens. Here's why that often backfires:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The first two weeks matter most.</strong> New listings get the most attention right away. An overpriced home misses that window.</li>
        <li><strong>Days on market become a signal.</strong> Buyers and agents notice stale listings and assume something is wrong.</li>
        <li><strong>Price drops don't reset the clock.</strong> A reduction after 45 days rarely brings back the interest you'd have had at the right price.</li>
        <li><strong>Anchoring works against you.</strong> Buyers compare your home to better-priced competition, and yours looks like the poor value.</li>
        <li><strong>Carrying costs add up.</strong> Mortgage, taxes, insurance, and utilities continue every month.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The opposite mistake, listing at the lowest price you'd accept, leaves money on the table and removes room to negotiate.</p>

      <h2>How We Help Sellers and Their Agents</h2>
      <p>For sellers with a listing agent, a <a href="/blog/pre-listing-appraisal-las-vegas">pre-listing appraisal</a> gives you an independent, supportable opinion of value to work from. It doesn't replace your agent's market knowledge. It backs it up with a USPAP-compliant analysis from a Nevada Certified Residential Appraiser. Agents often find it makes the pricing conversation easier, especially when a seller's expectations are higher than the comparable sales support.</p>

      <h2>Helping FSBO Sellers Set a Realistic Price</h2>
      <p>Selling without an agent means you're responsible for pricing, and online estimates aren't built for that. Automated valuations can't see your upgrades, condition, lot, or layout, and they often miss recent local sales.</p>
      <p>We analyze your home against recent comparable sales in your specific neighborhood and give you a clear, defensible value you can use to set your asking price.</p>
      <p>Many FSBO sellers use flat-fee services that place the listing in the MLS. That adds exposure, but listings that don't come with agent promotion often see fewer showings than agent-represented ones. That makes correct pricing even more important, because price is the strongest tool you have to attract buyers.</p>

      <h2>Other Advantages of a Pre-Listing Appraisal</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Fewer surprises at contract.</strong> When your list price is supported by the market evidence, there's less risk that the lender's appraisal concludes a value below the contract price, which can stall a deal or force a renegotiation. The appraiser doesn't set the value. The market does. A pre-listing appraisal shows you what the market is already saying.</li>
        <li><strong>Stronger negotiating position.</strong> You can respond to offers and counteroffers with documented market evidence.</li>
        <li><strong>Condition and repair insight.</strong> Understanding how condition and updates affect value helps you decide what's worth fixing before you list.</li>
        <li><strong>A neutral third party.</strong> For divorces, estates, and shared-ownership sales, an independent value can reduce disputes between parties.</li>
        <li><strong>Local expertise.</strong> We work Clark County every day, including Henderson, Summerlin, North Las Vegas, and Spring Valley.</li>
      </ul>

